  "notes": "The one entry here whose identity is a *timing* parameter rather than a timbre. The research documents that his drums are cut **to the sample** rather than under it — the fix on one of his beats was stretching and repositioning the sample so it \"align[ed] better with drums\" — and concludes: \"expect slight, deliberate misalignment as a groove feature.\" That is `humanize.quantizeStrength` 0.66 with `snare.offGridMs` 9 and a 4-11ms clap offset, which is the loosest grid in this batch outside the live-band and lo-fi entries. The second parameter is real harmony: \"4-bar loops with genuine chord motion rather than vamps\", with 7ths and 9ths carried over from the source, so 75% of `extensions` sits above the triad and `harmonicRhythm` is weighted 5:2 to one chord per bar — the opposite of the lane's static bed. Third, \"'infectious samples' are the whole melodic layer … with minimal added synth\": `timbreHint` is `chopped_vocal` and `chordToneBias` is high because the melody is the record's own harmony played back. ⚠ `medium` although the research says medium-high, because it also marks the tempo band itself [LOW-CONF]: \"gear/tempo not stated, tempo band inferred from his placements\". The INFERRED parameters are therefore `session.bpm` first and every drum placement second. ⚠ **\"Seven-minute drills\"** — beats built at speed, \"a space where you don't have time to think about it\" — is modeled as arrangement rather than as a note: single-idea, loop-static forms, `switchUpProb` 0.05, a short intro and a 4-bar outro. ⚠ The 120–135 half-time population is not modeled (one `halfTime` boolean, no modes authored).",
